    {
      "id": 1,
      "rank": 1,
      "unit": 1,
      "ar": "أَنَا",
      "en": "I / me",
      "pos": "pronoun",
      "note": "Lebanese has no present-tense 'to be'. أَنَا لُبْنَانِي is already a complete sentence — nothing goes between the two words.",
      "example": {
        "ar": "أَنَا مِن لُبْنَان.",
        "en": "I'm from Lebanon."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "rank": 2,
      "unit": 1,
      "form": "m",
      "pair": 3,
      "ar": "إِنْتَ",
      "en": "you (speaking to a man)",
      "pos": "pronoun",
      "note": "The fatha on the ت is the whole difference from the feminine. Lebanese often swallows it, but write it while you are learning.",
      "example": {
        "ar": "إِنْتَ مِن وَيْن؟",
        "en": "Where are you from?"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "rank": 3,
      "unit": 1,
      "form": "f",
      "pair": 2,
      "ar": "إِنْتِ",
      "en": "you (speaking to a woman)",
      "pos": "pronoun",
      "note": "Same word as the masculine with a kasra instead of a fatha. This kasra-for-fatha swap marks the feminine all through the language.",
      "example": {
        "ar": "إِنْتِ مِن وَيْن؟",
        "en": "Where are you from?"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "rank": 4,
      "unit": 1,
      "ar": "هُوِّي",
      "en": "he / it",
      "pos": "pronoun",
      "pron": "The shadda means you hold the و — two beats, not one.",
      "example": {
        "ar": "هُوِّي هَوْن.",
        "en": "He's here."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "rank": 5,
      "unit": 1,
      "ar": "هِيِّي",
      "en": "she / it",
      "pos": "pronoun",
      "note": "Lebanese uses هُوِّي and هِيِّي for objects too — every noun is either masculine or feminine.",
      "example": {
        "ar": "هِيِّي مْنِيحَة.",
        "en": "She's doing well."
      }
    },
    {
